ttention skills' support the attention requirement\". That is not a fact from notes. So we cannot rely on that unless it's a fact from discussion. It's a claim, but we can treat it as a derived observation. Might be okay.\n\nWe can ask for more info about Candidate C's crew cooperation or communication from other panel members? But we have the facts.\n\nWe can ask a tool to get more info from another panel member about Candidate D's hot temper effect? But we have that fact.\n\nWe need to produce a public message now. We can either maintain recommendation for C, or shift to B if we think communication issues are too severe. Let's weigh: C's egocentric and communication difficulty may affect crew cooperation, but he also \"puts concern for others above everything\" (not in original notes but Agent 3 said \"tendency to put concern for others above everything\". That is not a fact from notes; it's a derived statement. Not sure if we can use it. The original notes didn't include that. So we should stick to explicit facts: C keeps calm in crisis, understands complicated technology, can make correct decisions quickly, difficulty communicating ideas, regarded as egocentric, not willing to further education. No explicit crew cooperation fact. So we cannot claim crew cooperation positive. So C's crew cooperation is questionable due to egocentric and communication difficulty.\n\nB: very conscientious (reliability), handles stress well, good at assessing weather (technical), excellent computer skills (technical), makes nasty remarks (crew cooperation negative), weak memory for numbers (attention). So B also has crew cooperation issue.\n\nD: takes responsibility seriously (responsibility), solves problems extremely well (technical), can concentrate (attention), responds to unexpected events adequately (adaptability), hot temper (crew cooperation negative), know-it-all (crew cooperation negative). So D also has crew cooperation issues.\n\nA: leadership qualities (crew cooperation